30 lollipops. Jean eats 2 of the lollipops. With the remaining lollipops, Jean wants to package 2 lollipops in one bag. How many bags can Jean fill? The problem begins with 30 lollipops. Jean eats 2 of the lollipops, this means she eats 2 lollipops. The number of lollipops remaining is 30 - 2 = 28 lollipops. Jean wants to package 2 lollipops in one bag. The number of bags Jean can fill is the number of remaining lollipops divided by the number of lollipops per bag. Therefore, the number of bags is 28 / 2 = 14.
Thus, Jean can fill 14 bags.

